course: dictionary
lesson: 12137
title: Ordinary words, everyday meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Commonplace» means ordinary, something that happens often and is not surprising. «Phones were rare in Grandma's day, but now they are commonplace.» Lots of people have phones, so a phone is an everyday thing, not a surprise.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Snow is commonplace in the mountains in winter, so nobody was amazed when it fell on Tuesday.» The clue is nobody was amazed: if something is not surprising, it happens often. Try your meaning in the sentence: ordinary fits, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The word can also be a noun. A commonplace is something ordinary, or a remark said so often that it is no longer interesting. «"Have a nice day" is a commonplace» means it is a phrase everybody says, so it is no longer special.
fact: «Commonplace» means ordinary and happening often.
fact: Things that were once rare can become commonplace, like phones or computers.
fact: As a noun, «a commonplace» means something ordinary, or a remark that is said so often it is no longer interesting.
fact: «Commonplace» and «ordinary» are close in meaning, so you can often swap one for the other to check.
exercise: «Snow is commonplace in the mountains in winter.» What does commonplace mean here? | Cold and wet only at night | Rare and very exciting | *Ordinary and happening often | Hidden under the ground
exercise: Mia says, «How are you?» is a commonplace. What is a commonplace? | *Something ordinary that is said so often it is no longer interesting | A kind of cake | A bird that sings at night | A rare, exciting word
exercise: «Once, phones were rare. Now they are commonplace.» What does this tell us? | Phones are only for grown-ups | *Phones are ordinary now, because many people have one | Phones are hidden in the walls | Phones have become louder
test: Which word is closest in meaning to commonplace? | Unusual | Loud | *Ordinary | Secret
test: Lena says, «A commonplace event at our school is the bell at lunch.» What does commonplace mean in that sentence? | Very rare | *Ordinary and happening often | Very loud | Hidden
exam: «Zebras are commonplace on the plains of Africa, so nobody stopped to stare.» Why did nobody stop to stare? | Because the zebras were all asleep forever | Because zebras are invisible | *Because seeing zebras there is ordinary and happens often | Because zebras cannot be seen from the road
